      "ballot_id": "B024",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase reveal that quantum phase has observable consequences beyond local forces and energy levels. This is a profound, broadly influential insight into quantum mechanics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's work established the physical significance of electromagnetic potentials in quantum mechanics; Berry identified the geometric phase now bearing his name. Both contributions are distinct and central.",
        "maturity": "These results have been tested and applied across many areas of physics over decades; their significance is exceptionally well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The ballot combines two related but historically distinct results, so the precise scope of a prize would need care."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B044",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks made a major advance in precision timekeeping and opened tests of fundamental physics at unprecedented sensitivity. The work is both technically remarkable and scientifically consequential.",
        "attribution": "Katori developed the lattice clock concept and demonstrated its key advantages; Ye's group made major experimental advances in optical clocks and precision measurements. The two contributions are complementary.",
        "maturity": "The approach has been independently developed, refined, and adopted internationally. Its scientific value is clear and durable.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The broader clock field includes important contributions beyond the two names listed."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B028",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controlled quantum many-body systems experimentally accessible, including regimes relevant to Hubbard models. This is a major achievement in quantum simulation and fundamental many-body physics.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner led seminal experimental demonstrations of lattice systems and correlated phases; Zoller helped establish the theoretical framework for quantum simulation with atoms. Cirac's theoretical contributions are also important, making the three-person limit difficult.",
        "maturity": "The platform has produced a sustained body of increasingly precise work across laboratories. Some target condensed-matter questions remain challenging, but the experimental achievement is secure.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed discovery spans theory, platform development, and specific simulator results, complicating the boundaries of credit."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexandre Blais",
        "Andreas Wallraff",
        "Robert J. Schoelkopf"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit quantum electrodynamics brought cavity quantum optics into the solid-state domain and enabled strong, controllable interactions between microwave photons and superconducting qubits. It reshaped experimental quantum physics.",
        "attribution": "Blais developed the foundational theory for the circuit architecture; Wallraff and Schoelkopf led key experimental demonstrations and development. Girvin's theoretical role is substantial, so the credit boundary needs careful consideration.",
        "maturity": "The core effects are reproducible and the platform has supported a large, sustained research program. Its basic physics is established even as devices continue to evolve.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's success involves a large international community, and the proposed list leaves out a major theoretical contributor."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "Sajeev John",
        "John D. Joannopoulos"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals and photonic band gaps introduced a powerful way to control light through periodic structure, with deep conceptual links to electronic band theory and broad consequences for photonics.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated and developed the photonic band-gap concept; Joannopoulos helped build the theoretical understanding and establish the field. The listed names reflect complementary contributions.",
        "maturity": "The central concept has been confirmed and developed in many material systems over decades. Its lasting scientific and technological impact is clear.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The independent origins and subsequent development make a precise three-person attribution somewhat difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Bertrand I. Halperin",
        "David R. Nelson",
        "A. Peter Young"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The defect-based account of melting in two dimensions and the prediction of an intermediate hexatic phase changed the understanding of phase transitions in low-dimensional systems. It is a fundamental result in statistical physics.",
        "attribution": "Nelson and Halperin developed the central theory of defect-mediated melting, with Young making important contributions to its development and analysis. The three names are strongly associated with this work.",
        "maturity": "The theoretical framework is well established and has been tested in a range of two-dimensional systems, though realizations vary with interactions and conditions.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The extent to which particular experimental systems follow the idealized transition sequence is system dependent."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexander A. Belavin",
        "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov",
        "Alexander M. Polyakov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The development of two-dimensional conformal field theory provided exact tools for critical phenomena and influenced statistical physics and quantum field theory. Its intellectual reach is exceptional.",
        "attribution": "Belavin, Polyakov, and Zamolodchikov's work established the field's central framework and is rightly associated with all three.",
        "maturity": "The theory has had decades of deep use and development. Its mathematical foundations and importance to idealized critical systems are secure.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The connection between this theoretical framework and a single experimentally identified discovery may be less direct than for experimental candidates."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer",
        "Allan H. MacDonald"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene revealed tunable flat bands and unexpected correlated phases in a remarkably simple material system. The discovery opened a major new direction in condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald predicted the flat-band mechanism; Jarillo-Herrero's group demonstrated the striking correlated phenomena. All three made essential, distinct contributions.",
        "maturity": "Independent experiments have confirmed a rich set of phenomena, but the field is young and the microscopic explanations and relationship among phases remain unsettled.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The longevity and full conceptual significance of the observed phases are still being established."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
